\clearpage


\subsection{Variable Selection}

It is not feasible to test all of the variables in Table \ref{tab:candIV} 
simultaneously due to the presence of high correlation among some of these 
key indicators. In this section, I investigate the correlation among these 
variables and describe the process of reducing the list of factors in Table \ref{tab:candIV} 
to a manageable subset for inclusion in statistical models that can evaluate their 
impact. 

<<cleanupIV, echo=FALSE, results='hide'>>=
# library(GGally)
dist_turn$contest <- "Uncontested"
dist_turn$contest[dist_turn$nrealcand == dist_turn$nwins 
                  & dist_turn$ninc > 0] <- "Open Uncontested"
dist_turn$contest[dist_turn$nrealcand == dist_turn$nwins & 
                    dist_turn$ninc == 0] <- "Incumbent Uncontested"
dist_turn$contest[dist_turn$nrealcand > dist_turn$nwins 
                  & dist_turn$ninc > 0] <- "Incumbent Contested"
dist_turn$contest[dist_turn$nrealcand > dist_turn$nwins 
                  & dist_turn$ninc ==0] <- "Open Contested"

dist_turn$publicPupilCountLOG <- dist_turn$TOT_COUNT - dist_turn$TOTAL_COUNT_PVT
dist_turn$publicPupilCountLOG <- log(dist_turn$publicPupilCountLOG)
dist_turn$schoolMillShare <- dist_turn$millrate / (dist_turn$millrate +dist_turn$NonSDMill)
dist_turn$eqv_taxfiler <- dist_turn$eqv_adj_tifout / dist_turn$totalincome_count
dist_turn$OVERRIDE_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93[is.na(dist_turn$OVERRIDE_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93)] <- 0
# create a most recent partisanship variable

policyIdx <- c("NEW_SUP", "MATH_PROFADV_PER", "READ_PROFADV_PER", "debtFail", 
               "overrideFail", "overrideQues", "debtQues", "NonPublicPupilPer", 
               "ADMIN_SHARE", "ADMIN_SHARE_COMP", "debtPass", "overridePass",
               "DEBT_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93", "OVERRIDE_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93", 
               "SUP_TURNOVER_PRIOR_YEAR")

demogIdx <- c("per_white_all", "median_incomeLOG", "per65o", "VAP_adjLOG", 
              "OOH_share", "PerBachelorOrAbove", "publicPupilCountLOG", 
              "econ_disadv_per")

fiscalIdx <- c("PROPERTYTAX_REV_SHARE", "balance_member_lag", "TCEC_MEMBER", "TEC_MEMBER", 
               "NonSDMill", "millrate", "schoolMillShare", "millrate_delta", 
               "total_cat_aids_member", "teachSalDiff", "eqv_taxfiler")

#eqv_member

politicIdx <- c("recentTwoPartyShareDem", "incumRun", "nraces", 
                "partyDivisionRecent", "nwins", "teachShareofVoters", 
                "overrideYesPer", "fallTurnout")

# ggpairs(dist_turn[dist_turn$year == 2011 & !is.na(dist_turn$turnout),], 
#         columns = which(names(dist_turn) %in% fiscalIdx), 
#         lower = list(continuous = "smooth"), 
#         upper = list(continuous = 'cor'), 
#         title = "Pairs Plot of Key Control Variables")

nopanel <- theme(panel.background=element_blank(),
                 panel.grid.minor=element_blank(),
                 panel.grid.major=element_blank(),
                 axis.text.x = element_text(angle = -90, hjust = 0), 
                               axis.text = element_text(colour = "black"))
@

<<demog1, fig.cap="Correlations of Demographic Variables", fig.height=5.5, fig.width=7.5>>=
cormat <- melt(round(cor(dist_turn[!is.na(dist_turn$turnout), 
              which(names(dist_turn) %in% demogIdx)], use = "pairwise"), 3)) 

cormat$value2 <- cut(cormat$value, 
                     breaks=c(-1,-0.75,-0.5,-0.25,0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1), 
                     include.lowest=TRUE, 
            label=c("(-0.75,-1)","(-0.5,-0.75)","(-0.25,-0.5)","(0,-0.25)",
                    "(0,0.25)","(0.25,0.5)","(0.5,0.75)","(0.75,1)")) 

qplot(x=Var1, y=Var2, data=cormat, fill=value2, geom="tile", color = I("gray40")) + 
  scale_fill_brewer(palette = "RdYlGn",name="Correlation", 
                    breaks = levels(cormat$value2), drop =FALSE)+
  geom_text(aes(label = value), size = 2) + nopanel + 
  labs(x = "", y = "")
@

In Figure \ref{fig:demog1} we see a depiction of the correlations between key 
demographic variables with dark red and green representing the highest negative 
and positive correlations respectively.\footnote{These figures represent the correlation 
matrix for the complete pooled dataset across years and school districts. Correlations 
for individual years follow similar patterns, but are not shown. The Pearson correlation 
coefficient is reported.} The most problematic correlations are 
between the median household income and percentage of pupils in the school district 
eligible for free and reduced price lunch. The other large correlation is 
between the number of pupils in the district and the VAP. In the 
models below, I choose one variable from each of these pairs. Household median 
income is also strongly correlated with the proportion of the population with a 
BA or above and with the proportion of owner-occupied housing. As a result, 
it may be necessary to drop median income in favor of these two variables in the 
models below. 

