\section{Results}

I present results across three dependent variables for four models. I begin by 
discussing the results for each dependent variable separately, and then proceed 
to compare the results across models. For each model I examine both the statistical 
significance of predictors, but also the substantive meaning of the results for 
some of those variables. I do this by producing simulated observations and showing 
the prediction across the range of the variable of interest. 


<<cleanModData, results='asis'>>=
dist_turn$nonincumbRun <- ifelse(dist_turn$onlyIncum == 1, 0, 1)
dist_turn$minBlaisLago <- abs(dist_turn$minBlaisLago) #TODO fix negative BLQ

demogIdx <- c(demogIdx, "locale2")
dvs <- c("contestSer", "contestMinor", "contestMinorLag", 
         "contestSerLag", "incDefeats", "onlyIncum", "incumShare", "nonincumbRun", 
         "minBlaisLago")
ids <- c("distid", "year", "districtwide")



moddat <- dist_turn[, c(dvs, ids, demogIdx, fiscalIdx, policyIdx, politicIdx)]


moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)
moddat$contestSer <- factor(moddat$contestSer)
moddat$contestMinor <- factor(moddat$contestMinor)
moddat$contestMinorLag <- factor(moddat$contestMinorLag)
moddat$contestSerLag <- factor(moddat$contestSerLag)
moddat$onlyIncum <- factor(moddat$onlyIncum)
moddat$nonincumbRun <- factor(moddat$nonincumbRun)
moddat$locale2 <- factor(moddat$locale2)
moddat$incDefeats <- factor(moddat$incDefeats)
moddat$NEW_SUP <- factor(moddat$NEW_SUP)
moddat$SUP_TURNOVER_PRIOR_YEAR <- factor(moddat$SUP_TURNOVER_PRIOR_YEAR)
moddat$debtPass <- factor(moddat$debtPass)
moddat$overridePass <- factor(moddat$overridePass)
moddat$debtQues <- factor(moddat$debtQues)
moddat$overrideQues <- factor(moddat$overrideQues)
moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove <- moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove * 100
moddat$per_white_all <- moddat$per_white_all * 100
moddat$OOH_share <- moddat$OOH_share * 100
moddat$per65o <- moddat$per65o * 100
moddat$teachShareofVoters <- moddat$teachShareofVoters * 100

# Save scale parameters 
nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
ScaleMeans <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, mean, na.rm=TRUE)
ScaleSD <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, sd, na.rm=TRUE)
moddatScale <- cbind(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD); rm(ScaleMeans, ScaleSD)
#Rescale
library(arm)
nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)
moddat[, nums] <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, rescale)
@

\subsection{Presence of Non-incumbents}

<<glmmodsNI, results='hide'>>=

# moddat <- moddat[moddat$year != "2002" & moddat$year != "2003", ]

demogsNI <- glm(nonincumbRun ~ VAP_adjLOG + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + per_white_all +
              OOH_share + locale2 + year, data = moddat, family = "binomial") #bach and 65o switch?

demogSE <- get_CL_vcov(demogsNI, moddat$distid)
demogsNI$se <- sqrt(diag(demogSE))

politicNI <- update(demogsNI, . ~ . + overrideQues + overridePass + 
                      debtQues + debtPass +  partyDivisionRecent +
                    teachShareofVoters + fallTurnout + nraces) 
  # partyDivision and fallTwoPartyShareDem lose 2 years

politicSE <- get_CL_vcov(politicNI, moddat$distid)
politicNI$se <- sqrt(diag(politicSE))

fiscalNI <- update(demogsNI, . ~ . + schoolMillShare + PROPERTYTAX_REV_SHARE + 
                   TCEC_MEMBER + balance_member_lag + millrate_delta)

fiscalSE <- get_CL_vcov(fiscalNI, moddat$distid)
fiscalNI$se <- sqrt(diag(fiscalSE))

policyNI <- update(demogsNI, . ~ . + ADMIN_SHARE_COMP + NEW_SUP + NonPublicPupilPer + 
                   DEBT_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93 + OVERRIDE_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93)

policySE <- get_CL_vcov(policyNI, moddat$distid)
policyNI$se <- sqrt(diag(policySE))

fullmodNI <- update(politicNI, . ~ . +  schoolMillShare + PROPERTYTAX_REV_SHARE + 
                   TCEC_MEMBER + balance_member_lag + millrate_delta + 
                    ADMIN_SHARE_COMP + NEW_SUP + NonPublicPupilPer + 
                   DEBT_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93 + OVERRIDE_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93)


fullmodSE <- get_CL_vcov(fullmodNI, moddat$distid)
fullmodNI$se <- sqrt(diag(fullmodSE))
rescalenote <- "All continuous variables rescaled to center 0 
                and divided by 2 standard deviations."
stars.note <- apsrtable:::stars.note
coef.note <- "Year fixed effect coefficients excluded."

coefnames1 <- c("Intercept", "VAP", "\\% Over 65", "\\% Bachelor +", 
               "\\% White", "\\% OOH",  "Exurb", "Rural", "Rev. Lim. Attpt.", 
               "Rev. Lim. Pass", 
               "Bond Attpt.", "Bond Pass", 
               "\\% Polarized", "\\% Teach. Voters", "Fall Turnout", "Number Seats", 
               "\\% Millrate for Schl.", "\\% Rev. Prop. Tax", "Cost per Pupil", 
               "Bal. per Pupil", "Millrate $\\Delta$", "\\% Salary Admin.", 
               "New Sup.", "\\% Private School", "Cumul. Bond Att.", 
               "Cumul. Ref Att.")

glmNItab <-apsrtable(demogsNI, politicNI, fiscalNI, policyNI, fullmodNI, se = "robust", 
          float = "longtable",
          label="tab:glmModsNI", 
          omitcoef=list("year", 
                        expression(grep("year", coefnames, value = TRUE))), 
          model.names=c("Demog.", "Politic", 
                        "Fiscal",
                        "Policy", "Full"), 
          caption = "Models of Incumbent Lockout in School Board Elections",
          notes = list(stars.note, rescalenote, coef.note),
          Sweave=FALSE, digits = 3, coef.names = coefnames1)

@


\begin{tiny}
<<glmermodsNI, results='asis'>>=
library(optimx)
ctrl2 <- glmerControl(optimizer = "optimx", calc.derivs = FALSE, 
                     optCtrl = list(method = "nlminb", starttests = FALSE, 
                                    kkt = FALSE, maxit = 4000))
ctrl <- glmerControl(optimizer = "Nelder_Mead", calc.derivs = FALSE)



demogsMMni <- glmer(nonincumbRun ~ VAP_adjLOG + per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + per_white_all +
              OOH_share + locale2 + year + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, family = "binomial", control = ctrl) 

politicMMni <- update(demogsMMni, . ~ . + overrideQues + overridePass + 
                      debtQues + debtPass +   partyDivisionRecent +
                    teachShareofVoters + fallTurnout + nwins, control = ctrl2) 

fiscalMMni <- update(demogsMMni, . ~ . + schoolMillShare + PROPERTYTAX_REV_SHARE + 
                   TCEC_MEMBER + balance_member_lag + millrate_delta, control = ctrl)

policyMMni <- update(demogsMMni, . ~ . + ADMIN_SHARE_COMP + NEW_SUP + NonPublicPupilPer + 
                   DEBT_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93 + OVERRIDE_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93, control = ctrl)

fullmodMMni <- update(politicMMni, . ~ . +  schoolMillShare + PROPERTYTAX_REV_SHARE + 
                   TCEC_MEMBER + balance_member_lag + millrate_delta + 
                    ADMIN_SHARE_COMP + NEW_SUP + NonPublicPupilPer + 
                   DEBT_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93 + OVERRIDE_ATTEMPT_CUMUL93, 
                   control = ctrl2)


apsrtable(demogsMMni, politicMMni, fiscalMMni, policyMMni, fullmodMMni,
          float = "longtable",
          label="tab:glmerModsNI", 
          omitcoef=list("year", 
                        expression(grep("year", coefnames, value = TRUE))), 
          model.names=c("Demog.", "Politic", 
                        "Fiscal",
                        "Policy", "Full"), 
          caption = "Mixed Models of Incumbent Lockout in School Board Elections",
          notes = list(stars.note, rescalenote),
          Sweave=FALSE, digits = 3, coef.names = coefnames1)

@
\end{tiny}

The models in Table \ref{tab:glmerModsNI} are disappointing overall. Not only are 
most variables not statistically significant, many of them are not of the correct 
sign. The demographic variables fair poorly, although they are generally in the 
correct direction. The most consistent result is that the measure of the size 
of the community, voting age population, is positively related to a greater probability 
of non-incumbents participating in a race. The other variables that seek to control 
for the pool of candidates, however, are not statistically significant and some run 
counter to evidence from surveys showing board members are more highly educated, 
older, and less likely to be minorities. Thus, we would expect a greater share 
of these demographics in the community to be positively related to more non-incumbents. 

More disappointingly, the substantive variables provide relatively little detectable 
effects. The only variables that are statistically significant are the presence 
of a referenda attempt to exceed the revenue limit, a control for the number of 
seats open in the race, and the dollars per pupil in the district's savings account. 
These variables are all in the correct sign with a referenda attempt increasing 
the likelihood that non-incumbents will participate in the race, more seats open 
leading to more non-incumbents, and a larger fund balance being associated with 
decreased challenger participation in the election. 

% TODO: what does this add up to? 
% Non-significant variables correct?

\subsection{Non-Incumbents and More Candidates than Seats}

Table \ref{tab:glmerModsCon} shows the results for estimates of whether voters 
face a ballot with more challengers than seats and at least one non-incumbent 
on the ballot. This presents a better measure of what voters might think of as 
contestation because it is measuring the presence of an actual choice on the ballot 
for voters. These models are better supported by the variables I selected to 
test than the models predicting non-incumbents running - a positive sign. 

First, larger districts correctly are associated with more contestation, 
confirming the predictions about the importance of size posited by \citet{oliver12}. 
Another demographic variable shows that the greater the population over 65 in a community,  
the more likely voters will have a choice for school board election. This matches 
with the expectation that this represents a greater pool of potential candidates. 
The percentage of adults with a bachelor degree is inconsistently negative, suggesting 
that communities with greater levels of education in their population are less 
likely to experience school board choice. This is counter to our expectation but 
may reflect that these communities have greater consensus about the value and nature 
of locally provided education. 

Unsurprisingly, the measure for the number of incumbents is strongly positively 
related to the presence of more incumbents in the race decreases the likelihood 
the race will be contested. However, some policy decisions of school boards do 
appear to significantly affect the presence of new candidates for board - notably 
the decision to offer and the passage of an override referendum, the millrate 
for the district, and the community's history of recent bond referenda attempts. 
Attempting an override referendum in the prior year increases the likelihood of a 
challenge, but if an override referendum is successful, then this effect is negated. 
This suggests that the decision to offer an override referendum is politically perilous 
for school board members -- if they misjudge the community and the referendum fails, 
they increase their chance for a contested election. 

Even more support for fiscally minded voters emerges with a positive relationship 
between districts with higher tax rates and more historical bond referenda offered 
and likelihood of a challenge. School board races are more contentious when taxation 
rates rise and also as districts have offered more bond referenda. 


<<glmmodsContest, results='hide'>>=
